# Breaking changes

Prefer the better design. Happy to break and rebuild. One canonical surface per concern — no parallel Old/New APIs, no indefinite `#[deprecated]` shims, no soft half-migrations. Git history is the safety net (revert), not compatibility wrappers.

## Decision procedure

| Mode | Behavior |
|------|----------|
| Planning | Choose a clean break in the plan **or** ask when blast radius / product intent is unclear |
| Development **with** an approved plan | Follow the plan; do not re-litigate breakage mid-implementation |
| Development **without** a plan | Clear local tradeoff → clean cut; unclear / high blast radius → ask |

## No legacy surface (this repo)

Do **not** add or retain:

- Parallel **V1 / V2** (or `Old` / `New`) command shapes, types, or config keys side by side
- Indefinite deprecation shims or “compat” code paths after call sites are updated
- Version suffixes on the **current** canonical shape once the old path is gone

Keep old code **only** when it contains reusable logic not yet ported — then move that logic and **delete** the old entry point.

## Examples (tool)

```text
# ✅ GOOD — one command shape
Rename/replace a CLI flag or TUI form field in one change; update help and tests.

# ✅ GOOD — clean extension API
Replace an upsert/handler contract; update kernel + extension together.

# ❌ BAD — parallel versions
Old and new host-config keys both accepted indefinitely “for migration.”

# ❌ BAD — soft break
Deprecated alias that forwards forever.
```

After breaking work: update in-repo call sites, host-config docs when contracts change, and README when operator behavior changes.
